      <span class="hero-eyebrow">Module 8 · Lesson 2</span>
      <h1>Zero-Shot, Few-Shot, and <span class="accent">Chain-of-Thought</span> Prompting</h1>
      <p class="hero-sub">Master three fundamental prompting paradigms that enable LLMs to solve problems without fine-tuning. Learn when and how to use zero-shot prompting for immediate results, few-shot prompting with carefully selected examples, and chain-of-thought techniques that unlock complex reasoning capabilities.</p>
    </section>

    <div class="objectives">
      <h4>Learning Objectives</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>Understand the conceptual foundations and trade-offs of zero-shot, few-shot, and chain-of-thought prompting</li>
        <li>Apply zero-shot prompting for rapid task execution without example overhead</li>
        <li>Design high-quality few-shot prompts with examples that guide model behavior effectively</li>
        <li>Implement chain-of-thought reasoning to improve accuracy on complex reasoning tasks</li>
        <li>Combine multiple techniques into hybrid prompting strategies for optimal results</li>
        <li>Build Python applications using the OpenAI API to execute all three paradigms</li>
        <li>Evaluate and optimize prompts through systematic experimentation and comparison</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <section class="section" id="zero-shot-fundamentals">
      <span class="section-label">Foundations</span>
      <h2>Understanding Zero-Shot Prompting</h2>
      <p>Zero-shot prompting is the most fundamental interaction mode with language models. You provide a task description without any examples, relying entirely on the model's pre-trained knowledge to generate an appropriate response. Despite its simplicity, zero-shot prompting is surprisingly effective for a wide range of tasks because modern LLMs have learned extensive patterns across diverse domains during pre-training on billions of text tokens.</p>

      <p>The core strength of zero-shot prompting lies in speed and simplicity. You can immediately test hypotheses without investing time in example curation. This makes it ideal for prototyping, exploratory tasks, and one-off analyses. However, performance may suffer on highly specialized tasks, unusual output formats, or problems that require domain-specific knowledge.</p>

      <h3>When Zero-Shot Works Well</h3>
      <p>Zero-shot prompting shines for tasks the model encountered frequently during training:</p>
      <ul style="margin: 1rem 0 1rem 2rem;">
        <li><strong>General Question Answering:</strong> "What is the capital of France?" Zero-shot excels because the model has seen countless geography questions.</li>
        <li><strong>Text Summarization:</strong> The patterns for condensing text are well-represented in training data. Simple instruction suffices.</li>
        <li><strong>Sentiment Analysis:</strong> Models learn sentiment patterns extensively. A single instruction often generates good classifications.</li>
        <li><strong>Basic Translation:</strong> Language translation patterns are abundant in training data, enabling reasonable zero-shot performance.</li>
        <li><strong>Creative Writing:</strong> The model can generate stories, poems, and creative content from high-level instructions alone.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Basic Zero-Shot Prompt Structure</h3>
      <p>A zero-shot prompt typically includes three elements: a clear task description, the input to process, and optionally, guidance on output format.</p>

      <pre><code><span class="st">"Task: Classify the sentiment of customer reviews as positive, negative, or neutral."</span>
<span class="st">"Review: This restaurant has amazing food and fast service!"</span>
<span class="st">"Sentiment:"</span></code></pre>

      <p>This structure is intentionally minimal. By keeping instructions concise and direct, you avoid confusion and let the model apply its learned knowledge. Here's another example for a more complex task:</p>

      <pre><code><span class="st">"Extract the main subject and key verbs from this sentence."</span>
<span class="st">"Sentence: The rapidly growing startup has revolutionized"</span>
<span class="st">"the technology industry through innovative approaches."</span>
<span class="st">"Subject: startup, Verbs: growing, revolutionized"</span></code></pre>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Design Principle:</strong> In zero-shot prompting, clarity trumps length. One well-phrased sentence beats three confusing paragraphs. Let the model's pre-training do the heavy lifting.
      </div>

      <h3>Zero-Shot Limitations</h3>
      <p>Zero-shot prompting struggles in specific scenarios. If your task is niche, requires unusual output formatting, or involves domain jargon the model hasn't seen extensively, zero-shot may underperform. For example, asking a general LLM to classify specialized medical conditions without examples typically produces mediocre results because medical terminology is less common in general text corpora.</p>

      <p>Additionally, without examples, the model has no reference for the exact output format you want. It might provide a classification followed by explanation when you need just the label. This is where few-shot prompting becomes valuable.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="few-shot-learning">
      <span class="section-label">In-Context Learning</span>
      <h2>Few-Shot Prompting and Example Design</h2>
      <p>Few-shot prompting represents a major leap in capability and control. By providing 2-5 carefully crafted examples, you demonstrate the exact pattern you want the model to follow. The model then learns this pattern within the context window itself—this is called in-context learning. No weights are updated; instead, the model uses examples as a template for generating the target response.</p>

      <p>Few-shot prompting enables the model to adapt quickly to specialized domains, unique formats, and specific requirements. It's the practical choice for production systems where consistency and accuracy matter. The trade-off is modest: you invest time curating examples in exchange for significantly improved performance.</p>

      <h3>The Science of Example Selection</h3>
      <p>Research shows that few-shot example quality matters more than quantity. Five bad examples perform worse than two excellent examples. Here are evidence-based principles for example selection:</p>

      <ul style="margin: 1rem 0 1rem 2rem;">
        <li><strong>Domain Relevance:</strong> Examples should come from the same domain as your task. If classifying medical documents, use medical examples, not restaurant reviews.</li>
        <li><strong>Diversity:</strong> Ensure examples cover the full range of task variations. Include edge cases, typical cases, and boundary conditions.</li>
        <li><strong>Correctness:</strong> Every example must be correct. A single incorrect example can significantly degrade performance by teaching the wrong pattern.</li>
        <li><strong>Consistent Formatting:</strong> Use identical structure for each example. This makes the pattern obvious and easier for the model to replicate.</li>
        <li><strong>Balanced Coverage:</strong> If classifying into multiple categories, show examples from each category approximately equally.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Constructing a Few-Shot Prompt</h3>
      <p>Here's a well-structured few-shot prompt for text classification:</p>

      <pre><code><span class="st">"Classify customer support tickets as bug_report, feature_request,"</span>
<span class="st">"or documentation_issue."</span>

<span class="st">"Example 1:"</span>
<span class="st">"Ticket: The login button doesn't work on mobile devices."</span>
<span class="st">"Category: bug_report"</span>

<span class="st">"Example 2:"</span>
<span class="st">"Ticket: It would be helpful to export reports as PDF."</span>
<span class="st">"Category: feature_request"</span>

<span class="st">"Example 3:"</span>
<span class="st">"Ticket: The API docs don't explain rate limiting clearly."</span>
<span class="st">"Category: documentation_issue"</span>

<span class="st">"Ticket: The app crashes when uploading large files."</span>
<span class="st">"Category:"</span></code></pre>

      <p>Notice how this structure clearly delineates examples from the actual task. The consistent formatting (Ticket: [input], Category: [label]) creates an obvious pattern that the model can follow.</p>

      <h3>Few-Shot with OpenAI API</h3>
      <p>Here's how to implement few-shot prompting with Python:</p>

      <pre><code><span class="kw">from</span> <span class="nm">openai</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="nm">OpenAI</span>

<span class="nm">client</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">OpenAI</span><span class="op">(</span><span class="va">api_key</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"sk-your-key-here"</span><span class="op">)</span>

<span class="nm">few_shot_prompt</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">"""Classify emotional tone of customer feedback.

Example 1:
Feedback: Your support team is fantastic and responsive!
Tone: positive

Example 2:
Feedback: Product never works properly. Waste of money.
Tone: negative

Example 3:
Feedback: It works but has some issues.
Tone: neutral

Feedback: The new feature improved my workflow significantly!
Tone:"""</span>

<span class="nm">response</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">client</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="nm">chat</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="nm">completions</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">create</span><span class="op">(</span>
    <span class="va">model</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"gpt-4"</span><span class="op">,</span>
    <span class="va">messages</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="op">[</span>
        <span class="op">{</span><span class="st">"role"</span><span class="op">:</span> <span class="st">"user"</span><span class="op">,</span> <span class="st">"content"</span><span class="op">:</span> <span class="nm">few_shot_prompt</span><span class="op">}</span>
    <span class="op">],</span>
    <span class="va">temperature</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">0.0</span><span class="op">,</span>
    <span class="va">max_tokens</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">50</span>
<span class="op">)</span>

<span class="fn">print</span><span class="op">(</span><span class="nm">response</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="nm">choices</span><span class="op">[</span><span class="nm">0</span><span class="op">].</span><span class="nm">message</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="nm">content</span><span class="op">)</span></code></pre>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Practical Tip:</strong> Start with 2-3 examples. If performance is insufficient, add one carefully chosen example that represents a failure case. Rarely do you need more than 5 examples; beyond that, you may hit token limits or confuse the model.
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="chain-of-thought">
      <span class="section-label">Reasoning Technique</span>
      <h2>Chain-of-Thought Prompting for Complex Problems</h2>
      <p>Chain-of-thought (CoT) prompting is a transformative technique that explicitly asks the model to explain its reasoning process step-by-step before providing the final answer. Instead of generating a direct answer, the model articulates intermediate steps—breaking down complex problems into simpler components. This approach not only produces more accurate results on difficult reasoning tasks but also provides interpretability: you can see exactly how the model arrived at its conclusion.</p>

      <p>The power of chain-of-thought lies in a fundamental insight: by forcing intermediate reasoning, the model avoids taking reasoning shortcuts that lead to errors. When you ask a model to "think step by step," it engages a different cognitive pathway that's more deliberate and thorough than direct answering.</p>

      <h3>Zero-Shot Chain-of-Thought</h3>
      <p>The simplest CoT approach requires no examples. You simply add a prompt like "Let's think step by step" or "Show your reasoning:" to encourage intermediate steps:</p>

      <pre><code><span class="st">"If a car travels 60 km in 2 hours, how far will it travel"</span>
<span class="st">"in 5 hours at the same speed? Think step by step."</span></code></pre>

      <p>The model responds with something like:</p>

      <pre><code><span class="st">"Let me work through this:
- Speed = distance / time = 60 km / 2 hours = 30 km/hour
- Distance in 5 hours = speed × time = 30 km/hour × 5 hours = 150 km
- Therefore, the car travels 150 km in 5 hours."</span></code></pre>

      <p>This simple phrase triggers a more thorough reasoning process, significantly improving accuracy on math and logic problems.</p>

      <h3>Few-Shot Chain-of-Thought</h3>
      <p>Few-shot CoT combines examples with reasoning chains. You demonstrate how to solve similar problems with complete step-by-step explanations:</p>

      <pre><code><span class="st">"Solve word problems by showing detailed reasoning."</span>

<span class="st">"Example 1:"</span>
<span class="st">"Problem: A store has 3 shelves. Each shelf has 12 books."</span>
<span class="st">"How many books total?"</span>
<span class="st">"Solution: 3 shelves × 12 books/shelf = 36 books total."</span>

<span class="st">"Example 2:"</span>
<span class="st">"Problem: Maria buys 4 apples at $2 each and 2 oranges"</span>
<span class="st">"at $3 each. How much does she spend?"</span>
<span class="st">"Solution:"</span>
<span class="st">"- Apples: 4 × $2 = $8"</span>
<span class="st">"- Oranges: 2 × $3 = $6"</span>
<span class="st">"- Total: $8 + $6 = $14"</span>

<span class="st">"Problem: A recipe calls for 2 cups of flour for 12 cookies."</span>
<span class="st">"If I want to make 36 cookies, how much flour do I need?"</span>
<span class="st">"Solution:"</span></code></pre>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Research Finding:</strong> Studies show that chain-of-thought prompting improves reasoning accuracy by 30-50% on complex arithmetic and logic tasks. The improvement is even larger for harder problems.
      </div>

      <h3>Chain-of-Thought Implementation</h3>
      <p>Here's a complete implementation combining few-shot CoT with the OpenAI API:</p>

      <pre><code><span class="kw">from</span> <span class="nm">openai</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="nm">OpenAI</span>

<span class="nm">client</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">OpenAI</span><span class="op">(</span><span class="va">api_key</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"sk-your-key-here"</span><span class="op">)</span>

<span class="nm">cot_prompt</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">"""Solve reasoning problems step by step.

Example 1:
Problem: If x = 3 and y = 2, what is 2x + y?
Step 1: Substitute values: 2(3) + 2
Step 2: Calculate: 6 + 2 = 8
Answer: 8

Example 2:
Problem: A bag contains 5 red and 3 blue marbles.
If you draw 2 marbles without replacement, what's the
